# No host loops over blocks (spd_K)

Full development rules are in `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root. This is the one that
gets broken most often.

**Do not write `for (int b = 0; b < nblocks; b++)` in code that runs per step or
per stage.** Every meshblock carries the same element count regardless of its
refinement level, so a pack of blocks is rectangular and ONE launch spans all of
them. Use the whole-pack views (`pv.*`) with a `*_b` kernel (`sd_for_cells_b`,
`fv_for_cells_b`, `sd_min_cells_b`, `fv_for_cells_ngh_b`, ...). SD packs fold the
block into the leading axis as `b*n_ader`, FV packs as `b*n_var`.

Measured cost of getting this wrong, in this repo:

- FV halo exchanges: 7.81M launches / 131.5 s -> 606k / 28.5 s (4.6x)
- MHD advance phases: mixed-level advance 448.9 -> 80.2 s (5.6x)
- RK bookkeeping (save_state, copy_cons, cons_to_prim, combine, B_to_U,
  compute_dt): MHD AMR lane 92.19 -> 34.50 s at 1600 leaves (2.67x). These were
  74% of MHD's fenced time and 13.4x the hydro cost of the SAME work, only
  because the hydro side had already been batched and the MHD side sat in the
  `else` branch of the same `if constexpr`.
- MOOD detection: 21.3 s of a 24.5 s fenced total (87%), and invisible in every
  profile because they all pinned `mhd/mood_force_level=1`, where detection
  returns immediately.

## Do

```cpp
mhd_compute_primitives_b(pv.U_sp, pv.W_sp);          // one launch, all blocks
this->Dt = mhd_compute_dt_b(pv.W_cv, hx_p, hy_p, hz_p);
Kokkos::deep_copy(pv.U0_sp.Vector, pv.U_sp.Vector);  // blocks are slices of the pack
```

## Do not

```cpp
for (int b = 0; b < nblocks; b++) {                  // BAD in a per-step path
    mhd_compute_primitives(blocks[b].U_sp, blocks[b].W_sp);
    double db = mhd_compute_dt(blocks[b].W_cv, Xd[b].h, Yd[b].h, Zd[b].h);
    this->Dt = std::min(this->Dt, db);               // worse: a device->host sync per block
}
```

A per-block reduction that returns to the host is the expensive shape: it is a
fence per block per step. `mood_detect` ended in one, which is how 654 calls
became ~230k host syncs.

Legitimate exceptions, and nothing else: setup and regrid
(`build_block_solvers`), host-side scalar bookkeeping with no launches
(`sync_block_dt`), output-time diagnostics. Write a comment saying which.

**Keep the per-block path behind a switch and prove bitwise agreement.** Add a
`SPD_MHD_BATCH_MASK` bit (or `SPD_NO_RK_BATCH`-style flag), then md5 the dumps of
both paths on a MIXED-LEVEL mesh with the feature that exercises the code turned
on. Also put the new work inside a `PHASE()` scope, or it will not appear in
`SPD_PHASE_TIMES=1` and the next person to profile will optimise the wrong thing.
